<section>
    <p class="eyebrow">Step 2</p>
    <h3>Add the attribute to the test</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php" data-trim data-line-numbers="1-15|6|9-12">
use Symfony\Bridge\PhpUnit\Attribute\UseRecord;
use Symfony\Bundle\FrameworkBundle\Test\WebTestCase;

class GetRandomControllerTest extends WebTestCase
{
    #[UseRecord]
    public function testRandom(): void
    {
        $client = static::createClient();
        $client->request('GET', '/random');

        $this->assertResponseIsSuccessful();
    }
}
    </code></pre>
    <p class="fragment subtitle" style="font-size: 0.6em;">The test uses <code>GetRandomControllerTest/testRandom.har</code>, stored next to it. Nothing else changes in the code.</p>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Step two. A normal functional test. We call /random, and the controller calls an external API.</p>
        <p>[click] We add only one thing: the UseRecord attribute.</p>
        <p>[click] The rest does not change. No mock to inject. No service to replace.</p>
        <p>[click] The file name comes from the class and the method. It lives next to the test.</p>
        <p>And no configuration: the recorder is on by default in the test environment. A test without the attribute is not affected.</p>
        <p>Important: the attribute only gives the file name. It does not decide to record. That's decided when you run the tests.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
